    "seo": {
      "title": "Online Services in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Iran’s online service platforms, from ride-hailing to classified advertising, and the differences between their business models."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "different-platform-models",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Different platforms solve different problems",
        "content": "An online service is best understood by examining what happens after a user opens the interface. Tapsi describes its core offering as an online platform for requesting rides and couriers. Sheypoor presents a classified-advertising directory covering vehicles, property, employment, services, and consumer goods. These are different activities: requesting transport initiates a service request, while browsing an advertisement begins the search for a suitable seller, employer, property, or provider. [s1][s2]\n\nThis distinction helps international readers navigate the sector. A transport platform should be examined through the journey it coordinates; a classified service through the information it makes discoverable. An advertisement, a booking, and a completed service are separate events. Their volumes should not be added together as if they measured the same economic activity. This is an editorial framework for comparing the platforms, rather than a claim that they share one operating model. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "classifieds-and-access",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Classified advertising and access through the web",
        "content": "Sheypoor’s categories show how broadly a classified service can extend beyond second-hand household goods. Its inspected homepage includes employment, business services, industrial and commercial equipment, electronics, property, and vehicles. A single directory can therefore serve several distinct purposes: finding work, advertising professional services, locating equipment, or searching for a home. The existence of a category establishes the platform’s advertised scope; it does not establish the number or quality of transactions completed through it. [s2]\n\nThe interface also matters. Divar’s website explains access through a progressive web app, or PWA: a website that users can add to a phone’s home screen. Its instructions describe this option for both iOS and Android, without requiring a separate application download or manual app update. This provides a concrete example of how an Iranian platform offers access outside a conventional app-store installation. It does not establish that every feature works identically across devices. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "connectivity",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Connectivity is part of service infrastructure",
        "content": "Online services depend on more than their own application code. Cloudflare’s analysis of Iran’s partial internet restoration in May 2026 describes substantial interruptions to international connectivity and an uneven recovery. Its measurements also distinguish traffic from the announcement of internet address space: a network can remain visible in routing information while users’ ability to exchange data is severely restricted. [s4]\n\nFor understanding a service platform, the implication is methodological. A reachable homepage is not proof that registration, maps, payments, notifications, or customer support work throughout the country. Conversely, international traffic measurements alone do not demonstrate that every domestic service has stopped. Platform availability needs evidence from the relevant service and location. The network research supports treating connectivity as a constraint, without assigning unmeasured outage losses to individual businesses. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-this-branch",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "How to explore this branch",
        "content": "The Snapp and Tapsi pages examine individual transport platforms. Divar and Sheypoor belong to the classified-advertising side of the directory, while the home-service and booking branches require separate research into how providers are selected, appointments are confirmed, and responsibility is divided between the platform and the service provider. These branch labels organize the guide; they are not evidence that every listed model has the same scale or contractual safeguards.\n\nUseful comparisons should begin with precise questions: What does the platform undertake to arrange? Which part of the interaction remains between users? Does a reported number count accounts, requests, listings, or completed services? Answering those questions is more informative than treating all online activity as a single market. The inspected transport and classified pages support these distinctions, but do not provide a comprehensive measurement of Iran’s online-services economy. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
