    "seo": {
      "title": "Snapp: Transport and Everyday Services in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Snapp’s service ecosystem, reported passenger-trip activity, and the limits of company-published platform statistics."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "platform",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From transport to an everyday service interface",
        "content": "Snapp’s company profile dates its establishment to 2014 and presents the business as a super app: an interface bringing several services together. Its description includes transport, delivery, food, groceries, and travel. The useful distinction is between Snapp as a shared consumer interface and the particular service fulfilling a request. A passenger journey and a grocery delivery belong to different operating activities even when accessed through the same application. [s2]\n\nThe group’s reporting for Solar Hijri year 1403 separately describes Snapp Car, Snapp Box, food delivery, grocery shopping, credit services, and travel booking. The names therefore should not be treated as interchangeable measures of one transport business. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reported-scale",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading the reported scale",
        "content": "For Solar Hijri year 1403, Snapp reports passenger trips separately from registered and active accounts. Tehran had the largest reported city trip total, followed by Mashhad and Shiraz. Nationwide coverage consequently does not imply equal activity in every city. The annual trip observation below is a company-reported service count, not a count of unique people or all journeys made in Iran. [s1]\n\nAccount totals need similar care. An account registered at any point is different from an account active during a reporting year. Neither measure alone establishes how frequently a person travels, whether one person holds multiple accounts, or what share of household transport spending reaches the platform.",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reported-trip-table",
        "type": "table",
        "content": {
          "title": "Company-reported Snapp Car trips: verified annual observation",
          "dataset_id": "snapp-car-annual-trips"
        },
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "safety-and-accountability",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Safety functions and evidence of outcomes",
        "content": "Namnak’s account of the group’s 1404 report describes facial reauthentication for drivers, masked calling, route-deviation detection, and an emergency-contact function. These are reported tools and procedures. Their presence does not independently establish how quickly assistance arrives or whether risk has declined. [s3]\n\nThe same distinction applies to environmental claims. Namnak attributes estimated emissions savings from shared trips to Snapp. Such estimates require a counterfactual: what journeys would otherwise have occurred, in which vehicles, and over what distances. Without the underlying method, a reported estimate should not become an independently verified statement of environmental benefit. [s3]\n\nSnapp is consequently useful to study both as a service interface and as a producer of information about its own activity. Its reports document how it describes operations; independent evidence is still needed to evaluate worker conditions, consumer protection, and the effectiveness of safety systems.",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      }
